explicit assumption** (flagged [A#] inline and enumerated in Section 7). None of it should be read as fact. The design below is written so that each [A#] can be replaced with a confirmed value without changing the protocol structure.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Pre-registration\n\n### 1.1 Questions\n- **Q1 (primary):** Do models trained on Docta-cleaned labels achieve higher predictive performance on an independent human-adjudicated test gold than models trained on raw labels?\n- **Q2 (corroborating):** On the disagreement set D = {i : raw_i \u2260 cleaned_i}, do ChatGPT and in-house human referees favor the cleaned label over the raw label; and is ChatGPT directionally concordant with the human-adjudicated gold?\n\n### 1.2 Hypotheses\n- **H1 (primary):** \u0394macroF1 = macroF1(cleaned-arm) \u2212 macroF1(raw-arm) > 0 on the independent test gold. [A1: single-label classification; A2: test gold exists and is independent]\n- **H2 (primary for Q2):** The proportion of D instances where the adjudicated *human* gold matches the cleaned label exceeds the proportion matching the raw label by \u2265 0.10 absolute (pre-specified margin), tested with a one-sided binomial test at \u03b1 = 0.05. [A3: disagreement rate large enough; A4: annotation budget]\n- **H3 (secondary for Q2):** ChatGPT's modal verdict agrees with the human-adjudicated gold on \u2265 75% of D. [A5: ChatGPT available with fixed model version]\n\n### 1.3 Primary metric and claim rule\n- **Primary metric:** macro-F1 on the independent test gold (class imbalance assumed [A1]; accuracy, per-class precision/recall, and expected calibration error (ECE) reported as secondary).\n- **\"Demonstrably better\" (pre-specified claim rule):** cleaned labels are declared demonstrably better only if the **paired bootstrap 95% CI of \u0394macroF1 excludes 0**, with bootstrap resampling over test instances and seeds nested inside each resample. If the CI straddles 0, the result is **\"no demonstrated improvement\"** for Q1 regardless of the point estimate. D-restricted metrics (Section 4.2) are reported separately and **never pooled into, or used to override, the primary claim**.\n- **Effect size target:** pre-specified minimal detectable \u0394macrof1 \u03b4 = 0.03 at 80% power [A6] (see Section 5.1 for the resulting N_test).\n\n### 1.4 Stopping criteria (locked at pre-registration)\n- **Model training:** a fixed number of seeds (5) with identical hyperparameters; early stopping on the *validation* split only (never test). No adaptive/sequential significance-based stopping.\n- **Annotation:** D sample size fixed *before* annotation begins. Adaptive stopping is allowed only in one direction, pre-registered: if a 50-instance pilot yields IAA (Cohen's \u03ba) < 0.6 [A4], the annotation manual is revised, the pilot is re-run, and only then does full annotation proceed; the pilot instances are excluded from final analysis. Gold-check (attention) items are interspersed at 10% density.\n- **ChatGPT:** exactly 3 runs per instance (temp 0), fixed prompt template (Section 3.2); no re-prompting to \"fix\" answers.\n\n### 1.5 Pre-registration artifact\nThe protocol (this document), prompts/annotation manual, split seeds, metrics, claim rule, and stopping rules are frozen and timestamped before any annotation or training starts. [Suggestion \u2014 recommended, optional: publish the pre-registration at an OSF-style registry.]\n\n---\n\n## 2. Data, label sets, and the non-circular gold\n\n### 2.1 The two label arms\n- Every instance i in the corpus has **raw_i** [A7: existing noisy label] and **cleaned_i** [A8: Docta output, one label per instance, deterministic, label-side-only]. Feature vectors are identical across arms; only the target changes.\n- **Hard exclusion (validity gate):** the Docta cleaning transform must NOT consume the test split or any evaluation model's predictions. If this exclusion cannot be verified, Q1 is invalid and the design must be re-scoped \u2014 this is a screening criterion, not an assumption.\n\n### 2.2 Split\n- **Stratified split** by raw-label class (e.g., 70/15/15 train/val/test) [A1, A9: no existing protocol; we create it]. Both arms keep the same instances in all splits, so the model arms differ only in target.\n\n### 2.3 Independent test gold (non-circular)\n- The test split receives a **separately produced, human-adjudicated gold label**: annotators see only the source item (no candidate labels, no raw/cleaned provenance), which prevents anchoring. This gold is the single reference for Q1.\n- **Cost/feasibility assumption stated as such [A2]:** producing this gold is the dominant cost. It is required for a non-circular Q1.\n- **Degraded fallback (suggestion, NOT required):** if independent test gold is unaffordable, Q1 degrades to \"cleaned-as-proxy\" evaluation on the whole test set, with D adjudicated via Section 3; this is circular (trivially favors cleaned) and must be labeled \"degraded, non-decisive\" \u2014 never presented as the primary result.\n- **Why not use cleaned as test gold:** that would make H1 satisfied by construction (label leakage). The entire design is built around avoiding this [researcher-approved requirement].\n\n---\n\n## 3. Disagreement-set (D) protocol \u2014 identical for both referees\n\n### 3.1 Definition and sampling\n- **D = {i : raw_i \u2260 cleaned_i}.** Disagreement rate r = |D|/N is a headline statistic [A3].\n- If |D| > N_D_target (Section 5.1), **stratified-sample N_D** from D (strata: class and direction-of-conflict) before adjudication; otherwise adjudicate all of D. Sampling is seeded and frozen at pre-registration.\n- Referees adjudicating D do NOT see whether each candidate is raw or cleaned (provenance-blind).\n\n### 3.2 Decision schema (used identically by humans and ChatGPT)\nEach D instance is presented with the source item and two candidates, labeled only \"A\" and \"B\" in **randomized order** (position must not correlate with provenance). Verdict options (locked):\n1. \"A is correct, B is wrong\" \u2192 A\n2. \"B is correct, A is wrong\" \u2192 B\n3. \"Both valid\" (acceptable)\n4. \"Neither valid\" \u2192 referee supplies a **third correct label** (free text from a fixed closed label set [A1])\n\n- **Humans:** \u2265 2 in-house annotators per instance, each using an annotation manual with the schema above plus label definitions; IAA via Cohen's \u03ba (2 annotators) / Krippendorff's \u03b1 (if >2); disagreements resolved by a senior adjudicator (or majority vote with senior tie-break) \u2192 **g_H** on D. Pilot (50 instances, Section 1.4) before full annotation. Gold-check items at 10% density. [A4]\n- **ChatGPT:** same source item + same A/B candidates and same decision schema, delivered through a **templated prompt** that embeds the annotation manual verbatim and requests **parseable JSON** (`{\"verdict\": \"A\"|\"B\"|\"both\"|\"neither\", \"third_label\": ...}`). Temperature 0; exactly **3 runs** per instance. Outcomes: per-run verdicts, **modal verdict** g_C, and **self-agreement** (proportion of instances where all 3 runs agree; reported explicitly, and instabilities excluded from the primary Q2 tables but shown separately). [A5]\n\n### 3.3 What the referees provide\n- **g_H** (human-adjudicated gold on D) and **g_C** (ChatGPT modal verdict on D), each expressed in the A/B/both/neither schema.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Analysis plan\n\n### 4.1 Q1 \u2014 whole-test performance\n- Train identical model(s) on raw-arm and cleaned-arm (same architecture, hyperparameters, seeds, early stopping on val). Base configuration: **TF-IDF + logistic regression** [suggestion: also add one strong model, e.g., a fine-tuned transformer, as a robustness arm, subject to compute/budget A10].\n- **Report:** point estimates (accuracy, macro-F1, per-class P/R, ECE) per arm; **\u0394macroF1 with paired bootstrap 95% CI over instances with seeds nested**; per-seed spread; McNemar test on accuracy as a complement to the primary CI rule.\n- **Claim:** apply Section 1.3 rule to the CI.\n\n### 4.2 Q2 \u2014 D-restricted analyses (reported separately, NEVER pooled with 4.1)\n- **Human preference:** proportion of D where g_H matches cleaned vs raw; one-sided binomial test vs proportion 0.50 with the pre-specified 0.10 margin (H2). Also \u03c7\u00b2-style check that \"both valid\" / \"neither\" rates are comparable across arms.\n- **ChatGPT preference:** same statistics using g_C (H2's ChatGPT analogue).\n- **Referee concordance:** ChatGPT\u2194human agreement on D (against g_H). If ChatGPT and humans **conflict in direction** \u2014 i.e., humans favor one label while ChatGPT favors the other \u2014 the report states **per-referee conclusions separately** and makes no combined \"both validated\" claim. Per the approved plan, the Q1 performance result is the primary claim; Q2 corroborates it and is reported per-referee on conflict.\n- **Model correctness on D:** both trained models' accuracy on D against **g_H** \u2014 this directly tests *\"cleaned helps predict exactly the instances that conflict,\"* where whole-test differences may be diluted by easy instances. Reported separately.\n- **Decision-schema breakdown:** rows for A-correct / B-correct / both-valid / neither, per referee.\n\n### 4.3 Controls and confound handling (summary)\n1. **Isolation of the label variable:** identical features/splits/seeds/hyperparameters; only the target differs.\n2. **Non-circularity:** independent test gold (Section 2.3) + cleaning-consumption exclusion (Section 2.1).\n3. **Blinding:** adjudicators never know raw-vs-cleaned provenance; candidate order randomized (Section 3.2).\n4. **Class-prior shift:** compare label distributions raw vs cleaned (e.g., two-sample \u03c7\u00b2 / balance metrics). If cleaning shifts priors, report **per-class** performance and state whether any gain is explicable as a prior artifact rather than label correctness.\n5. **Subpopulation separation:** whole-test (4.1) and D-restricted (4.2) are never pooled.\n6. **Annotator/referee quality:** IAA gates (\u00a71.4), gold-check items, senior adjudication, ChatGPT self-agreement, fixed prompt/temperature.\n7. **Statistical discipline:** pre-registered metric, claim rule, stopping rules; paired tests and bootstrap CIs over seeds **in addition to** point estimates.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Feasibility (design reasoning \u2014 NOT executed results)\n\n### 5.1 Sample-size / power reasoning [A6, A3]\n- **N_D for H2 (binomial):** detecting a Q2 preference of 60/40 vs 50/50 (one-sided, \u03b1 = 0.05, power = 0.80) requires **N_D \u2248 194** (computed: n = (z_{.975}\u221a(p0(1\u2212p0)) + z_{.80}\u221a(p1(1\u2212p1)))\u00b2/(p1\u2212p0)\u00b2 = (1.96\u00b70.5 + 0.8416\u00b70.49)\u00b2/0.01 \u2248 194). With r \u2248 0.10 [A3], this needs a corpus of \u2248 2,000 instances; the required corpus scales as 1/r.\n- **N_test for H1:** for \u03b4 = 0.03 macro-F1 at 80% power with paired analysis [A6], the required test size depends on the *paired* variance of per-instance correctness; an illustrative planning figure is N_test \u2248 500\u20131,000 (state the assumed worst-case paired \u03c3 before collection). These are **planning heuristics that calibrate budget and data needs**, not experimental findings. The real N is finalized with a paired pilot on a 100-instance slice once real data exist.\n\n### 5.2 Synthetic smoke test (pipeline validation; to be run only after approval of an execution step, not performed here)\nOutline to prove the evaluation stack runs and yields non-degenerate outputs:\n1. Generate a small synthetic classification corpus (e.g., 2,000 instances, 5\u201310 classes) with injected label noise; simulate a mock \"cleaner\" (e.g., majority vote over simulated noisy re-annotations) to produce a cleaned set and hence D.\n2. Run the full split + train pipeline (TF-IDF + LR, both arms, 5 seeds); emit all Q1 metrics and the paired-bootstrap CI code on the synthetic gold.\n3. Simulate human adjudication (rule-based mock) and ChatGPT verdicts (mock JSON) on sampled D; compute IAA, binomial tests, concordance, and class-prior checks end-to-end.\n4. Gate checks: CI code runs and is non-degenerate; D \u2265 the N_D target under the mock disagreement rate; JSON parsing of verdicts is lossless; IAA computation handles ties.\nThis is **design/feasibility reasoning only**; environment check confirms Python 3.14.4 is present and no project dependencies are preinstalled, so a dependency-free evaluation stack is the plan. **No experiment has been run.**\n\n---\n\n## 6. Deliverables / reporting structure\n1. **Pre-registration record** (\u00a71) \u2014 frozen before execution.\n2. **Q1 table:** arm-wise metrics, \u0394 with CIs, per-seed spread, McNemar.\n3. **Q2 tables (separate):** human-agreement-with-cleaned vs raw on D; ChatGPT-agreement; referee concordance; per-referee conclusions; D-restricted model correctness vs g_H.\n4. **Class-prior-shift report** and the prior-artifact discussion.\n5. **Feasibility/power memo** from \u00a75, plus synthetic smoke-test log (if an execution step is later approved).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Assumptions (all flagged, not facts) [cross-referenced from above]\n- **A1** Single-label classification; closed label set; stratification possible.\n- **A2** An independent human-adjudicated test gold is affordable/available (dominant feasibility cost).\n- **A3** Disagreement rate r \u2265 ~0.10, making D powered for H2.\n- **A4** Annotation budget: \u22652 annotators + senior adjudicator + pilot/gold-checks; IAA threshold 0.6.\n- **A5** ChatGPT available with a fixed model version and a small per-instance prompt budget; 3 runs feasible.\n- **A6** \u03b4 = 0.03 macro-F1 at 80% power is the agreed minimal effect.\n- **A7** Raw labels are pre-existing, noisy, per-instance labels; single label per instance.\n- **A8** Docta is deterministic, label-side-only, producing one cleaned label per instance.\n- **A9** No existing train/val/test protocol exists; we create the stratified split.\n- **A10** Compute/budget for the optional second (strong) model arm; not required for the base design.\n\n## 8. Uncertainties to resolve before any execution (if the task later moves to execution)\nReal dataset identity/size/classes; existence and cost of g_H; actual r and |D|; exact Docta transform and leakage audit; raw-label provenance (do in-house annotators co-author raw labels?); human annotation budget; ChatGPT version/access policy; choice of evaluation model portfolio.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Closing statement\n**This is a design-only deliverable prepared under the task contract (\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"). No experiment was run, no dataset was processed, no model was trained, and no result or outcome is claimed. All quantitative values herein (e.g., N_D \u2248 194, \u03b4 = 0.03, IAA threshold 0.6) are pre-registered planning parameters or illustrative arithmetic, not measured findings.**", "evidence": "Workspace re-verified unchanged: /workspace contains only task.json (track.md text: \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"). Filesystem search found no dataset/Docta/annotation material; Python 3.14.4 present, no preinstalled dependencies. Illustrative power arithmetic verified with python3: binomial N_D \u2248 194 for p0=0.5 vs p1=0.6, alpha=0.05, power=0.80. These are design/planning computations, not experimental results. No experiment was executed.", "assumptions": ["A1: task is single-label classification on a closed label set with stratifiable data", "A2: independent human-adjudicated test gold is affordable/available (dominant feasibility cost)", "A3: disagreement rate r between raw and cleaned labels is >= 0.10 so D is powered", "A4: annotation budget sufficies for >=2 annotators, senior adjudicator, pilot (IAA>=0.6) and 10% gold-check items", "A5: ChatGPT is available with a fixed model version and prompt budget allowing 3 temp-0 runs per instance", "A6: agreed minimal detectable effect delta=0.03 macro-F1 at 80% p